    passageText: `The power to amend the Constitution under Article 368 is a constituent power, not an ordinary legislative power; yet as settled in [1], the word "amend" does not encompass the power to abrogate, emasculate, or destroy the basic structure or essential features of the Constitution. When Parliament enacted the First Constitutional Amendment inserting Article 31B and the Ninth Schedule, the original object was to immunize agrarian land reform statutes from challenge on the ground of inconsistency with Part III. However, over subsequent decades, scores of enactments wholly unrelated to agrarian reform were placed inside the Ninth Schedule as a constitutional vault against judicial scrutiny.

In [2], a Nine-Judge Constitution Bench was called upon to determine the nature and extent of immunity enjoyed by laws inserted into the Ninth Schedule after 24th April 1973—the date on which [1] was pronounced. The Court unanimously held that the power to grant absolute constitutional immunity to legislation by a simple device of Ninth Schedule inclusion is incompatible with the doctrine of basic structure and judicial review.

Consequently, every constitutional amendment enacted on or after 24th April 1973 by which a statute is inserted into the Ninth Schedule must be tested by reference to the essential features of the Constitution as reflected in Articles 14, 19, and 21, read with the principles underlying Part III. The Court formulated a two-step inquiry: first, whether the law violates any fundamental right in Part III; and second, if it does, whether the transgression damages or destroys the basic structure in terms of the "rights test" (essence of rights test) and the "direct impact and effect test" (as distinct from the "form of amendment test"). Earlier, in [3], the Court had struck down Clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368 (inserted by the 42nd Amendment), famously observing that the Indian Constitution is founded on the bedrock of the balance between Part III and Part IV, and that a limited amending power is itself one of the basic features of the Constitution.`,
