    citation: "(1984) 4 SCC 116 (3-Judge Bench) & AIR 1958 SC 22",
    passageText: `In criminal trials where there is no direct ocular witness to the commission of the homicide, the prosecution case rests entirely upon circumstantial evidence and, where available, a dying declaration recorded under Section 32(1) of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 (now Section 26(a) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023). In [1], a Three-Judge Bench of the Supreme Court synthesized five golden principles—popularly revered in Indian criminal jurisprudence as the "Panchsheel" of proof of a case based on circumstantial evidence:

(1) The circumstances from which the conclusion of guilt is to be drawn should be fully established; the circumstances concerned "must or should" and not "may be" established;
(2) The facts so established should be consistent only with the hypothesis of the guilt of the accused, that is to say, they should not be explainable on any other hypothesis except that the accused is guilty;
(3) The circumstances should be of a conclusive nature and tendency;
(4) They should exclude every possible hypothesis except the one to be proved; and
(5) There must be a chain of evidence so complete as not to leave any reasonable ground for the conclusion consistent with the innocence of the accused and must show that in all human probability the act must have been done by the accused.

Furthermore, [1] examined the scope of Section 32(1) of the Evidence Act, contrasting the broad Indian expression "any of the circumstances of the transaction which resulted in his death" (as interpreted by the Privy Council in [2]) with the narrower English common law rule requiring the declarant to be under a settled, hopeless expectation of imminent death. Regarding the evidentiary weight of a dying declaration, the foundational Constitution Bench decision in [3] and the five-principle summary in [4] settled that a dying declaration is substantive evidence and can form the sole basis of conviction without corroboration if the Court is satisfied that it was completely voluntary, truthful, and made while the deceased was in a fit state of mind.`,
